      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "μᾶλλον δύνασθαί τινα πλεῖον νοεῖν",
          "english_quote": "someone could understand more",
          "issue": "The draft represents πλεῖον with “more,” but does not separately preserve μᾶλλον qualifying the ability expressed by δύνασθαι. The surviving Greek distinguishes greater ability from greater understanding.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use wording such as “someone is better able to understand more,” retaining the following six-dot and three-dot gaps exactly as supplied. The revised wording requires fresh review; the printed gaps themselves are already faithfully represented and do not require restoration.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "εἰ δ’ ἄρα τις βουληθείη πυνθάνεσθαι τὸ τίς ἂν ὁ φανείς, δύναται λέγειν ἑαυτῷ ὃ βούλεται.",
          "english_quote": "If someone should wish to inquire who the one appearing is, the apparition can say whatever it wishes about itself.",
          "issue": "The English selects the apparition as the unstated subject of δύναται and interprets ἑαυτῷ through the preceding identity question as self-description. That contextual reading is defensible, but the note’s alternative “for itself” does not disclose the materially different “to himself” reading, under which the questioner can tell himself whatever he wishes. The printed wording leaves both the subject and the force of the dative open.",
          "suggested_revision": "Expand the note to explain that the main rendering takes the apparition as subject and understands the dative as referring to speech on its own behalf, with self-description supplied by the identity-question context. Preserve the alternative: “If someone wishes to inquire who the appearing figure is, he can tell himself whatever he wishes,” taking the questioner as subject. Do not present “about itself” as the literal meaning of ἑαυτῷ or silently replace the printed reflexive.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "17.15",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": true,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἀλλ’ οὖν γε τὰ ὁράματα καὶ τὰ ἐνύπνια θεόπεμπτα ὄντα οὐ ψεύδεται, περὶ ὧν ἂν εἰπεῖν θέλῃ.",
          "english_quote": "nevertheless visions and dreams, being sent by God, do not lie about whatever he wishes to say.",
          "issue": "The draft’s “he” naturally identifies God as the subject of θέλῃ. This is a defensible contextual interpretation, but the Greek does not expressly name that subject, and no note discloses the alternative. The visions and dreams can also be the subject: neuter plural subjects permit singular verbal agreement, as the immediately preceding ψεύδεται demonstrates.",
          "suggested_revision": "Add a source-quoted note explaining that “he” is understood as God, the sender identified by θεόπεμπτα. Preserve the alternative “about whatever they wish to say,” taking the visions and dreams as the subject of θέλῃ with neuter-plural singular agreement. This is interpretive uncertainty, not evidence of a defective transcription.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    }
